# 1. Outcome and scope

Bram, the owner's persistent warrior companion, adventures in the owner's party,
uses a local LLM to choose bounded tactics, records real outcomes, and gradually
retains useful tactical changes across logout and server restart. Evaluation runs
automatically alongside normal play; the owner need not operate a separate lab.

The first learnable behavior is ability priority: compare the established warrior
selection with a finite, capability-compatible priority plan over Bram's currently
learned legal abilities. The model proposes a plan and evidence-backed revisions;
C++ executes actions, computes outcomes, and decides whether evidence qualifies a
revision for retention. This is persistent memory and policy adaptation, not
model-weight training.

A prerequisite is a bounded combat-policy refactor: expose a runtime playbook to
the existing ability selection and execution paths before connecting learning.
The LLM proposes validated data, never edits C++, recompiles the server or invents
new executable mechanics. Version 1 permits only a finite ordering and declared
gates for capability-catalog actions; it does not permit arbitrary conditions,
targets, spell IDs, or novel class behavior.

User-authorized work in this session is this specification. Implementation is
handed off through the bounded cards below; this document does not deploy changes,
alter live character records, accept Phase 2, or authorize one unbounded worker.

Required experience:

- Bram stays in the owner's existing party and works with .botinit and owner orders.
- Learning begins with observation, then shadow evaluation, then low-risk trials.
- Status explains what is being tested, why, evidence count, uncertainty and rollback.
- Lack of an applicable experiment is normal: Bram keeps playing his baseline.
- Other companions keep their existing behavior. World population is not required.

Excluded: general quest autonomy, new pulls, PvP, autonomous travel, free gear or
spells, intentional mistakes, chat-history capture, cloud fallback, weight training,
arbitrary model-generated code, SQL, rules or rotations, and cross-character learning.

