# 3. Capability audit and first experiment

BL-001 is complete: `reserve_rage` is NOT viable as specified. See
`bram-party-learning-bl001-audit.md`. Do not revive that candidate unless a later,
separately accepted capability and metric make it causal. Its findings do not mean
Bram must wait for level 10: at every level the catalog exposes only abilities he
actually knows and can legally execute. Enrollment resolves the owned character
once and stores its stable identity locally; it never changes level or spells.

Audit Assist, ContinueCombat and Defend separately below and above the declared tank
gate. Document which selector runs, whether it spends rage, and which decisions a
reserve can change. The existing tank Assist branch is Taunt/ordinary-attack only;
adding a reserve to the generic selector must not be presented as tuning that branch.
Include the actual .botinit follow/defend path in the acceptance matrix.

First playbook vocabulary:

- baseline: the exact current per-class selection order and eligibility checks.
- rotation_plan: an ordered subset of catalog action keys plus one declared gate
  per action from a finite vocabulary: always-eligible, target-aura-missing,
  resource-at-least-cost, range-valid, melee-reachable, or cooldown-ready.
- A plan may reorder or omit only actions available in Bram's current capability
  fingerprint. It cannot add target selection, new pulls, stance changes, gear,
  other companions, raw spell IDs, or conditions outside the catalog.
- The model proposes `baseline` or the preassigned trial plan; trial assignment,
  not the model, determines which eligible encounter exposes a candidate.
- A candidate changes one declared priority/gate difference from its parent. All
  other plan entries and safety behavior remain fixed, making outcome attribution
  possible.

BL-001R must audit the first current-level rotation candidate against the catalog
and matching game data. It must name the capability fingerprint, legal action keys,
the one changed priority/gate, an expected causal mechanism, and a metric bundle.
If no candidate can be measured honestly, retain baseline and continue observation.
Queued on-next-swing abilities remain unsupported unless their lifecycle is repaired
and independently accepted in a separate prerequisite. Never silently unlock them.

Immediate owner hold, recovery, target legality, existing attack scope and emergency
threat recovery are immutable constraints. This experiment does not delay Taunt to
manufacture a tradeoff. Existing owner-only protection is not represented as full
party tank intelligence. Record non-owner victim time as a metric; expanding threat
recovery to all party members is separate work if needed.

# 4. Runtime architecture and model use

## 4.1 Shared combat-policy prerequisite

Introduce these responsibilities with explicit value contracts; final file names
are selected in the bounded architecture card, not mandated as an engine rewrite:

| Component | Contract | Reuse boundary |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Ability catalog | Supported action keys, verified effects, resource units/cost semantics, target types and restrictions; resolve current learned ranks and live availability separately | Shared format, class-specific declarations |
| Runtime playbook | Immutable schema/version, class/role/capability compatibility, baseline priorities and finite catalog gates | Shared format; initial LLM mutation permits only warrior catalog priority/gate changes |
| Decision selector | Consume observation, capability set, playbook and engagement source; produce typed action/target plus decision reason | Shared eligibility machinery with explicit role policies |
| Executor | Re-resolve targets, enforce live legality and orders, perform normal engine actions and report outcomes | Existing integration reused; no async engine pointers |

Conceptual interface (not a mandated C++ signature):

    Decision SelectAction(Observation, CapabilitySet, Playbook, EngagementContext)

Separate declared capability from current executability: learned does not imply
supported; supported does not imply affordable, in range or off cooldown. Descriptions
sent to the model and action keys accepted by the executor must derive from the
same versioned catalog. Resource-unit conversion is explicit per power type.

Preserve original per-class action order and all eligibility checks during extraction.
Use the baseline playbook when disabled, incompatible, missing or invalid. Unknown
classes/settings fail back to their existing behavior, never an arbitrary class plan.
Owner priority and emergency constraints live outside tunable playbook settings.

Route Assist, ContinueCombat and Defend through the common policy interface while
preserving their source-specific restrictions and existing role branches. Sharing
an interface does not authorize changing which actions each source permits. Explicit
compatibility profiles may retain current differences. Record selected policy and
whether its setting was applicable on every evaluated action; no-effect exposure
must not count as evidence that a learned policy worked.

Do not implement a generic expression language, script engine, model-generated
conditions or arbitrary priority editing in this version. A future priority-plan
feature needs its own finite condition vocabulary and behavioral acceptance.

## 4.2 Other classes

Planner transport, memory/versioning, recorder infrastructure, evaluation lifecycle
and owner commands must be reusable without copying the warrior implementation.
Schemas carry class/role/capability identifiers, not hardcoded names or warrior-only
resource fields. Role-specific metrics and supported settings remain explicit.

- Warrior is the only live learning implementation in this release.
- Priest healing requires friendly-target healing/triage support; its generic
  offensive spell list and the druid healer policy do not provide that behavior.
- Mage extensions require tested ranged/CC behavior and spell priorities.
- Hunter extensions require tested ranged/melee transitions and any pet mechanics
  they advertise. Listing a shot in the catalog is not proof of complete execution.
- Reuse the framework for a second existing class in value-level baseline-equivalence
  tests (mage is sufficient). This tests the contracts without adding live learning
  or promising complete class support. No second-class runtime feature is in scope.

## 4.3 Model and runtime flow

World snapshot -> async planner -> constrained tactic -> authoritative executor
-> encounter summary -> async reflection -> candidate -> coded evaluation -> playbook.

The world thread performs bounded observation/recording and nonblocking queue
operations. It never waits for inference or learning-storage I/O. No raw Unit
pointers cross async boundaries. Implement a bounded persistence worker using the
repository's supported database threading/connection conventions.

Observation v2 includes schema, request/session/login/order generations, server
capture time, capability fingerprint, policy version, role, health/rage, current
engaged hostile references, victim/threat facts, and supported ability facts
(learned, cost, cooldown, stance requirements, range and legal target types).
Use at most five party slots, two eligible hostile slots and eight supported
ability slots. Larger encounters are ineligible for trials, not truncated into an
apparently safe two-enemy encounter. Mark unsupported/missing facts explicitly.

The sidecar receives opaque session references, never account identifiers, private
character dumps or raw chat. The game owns stable identity mapping. Local runtime
combat telemetry is explicitly part of this feature; it is not permission to send
personal game records to coding workers or retrieval services.

Planner input adds the accepted playbook and at most three applicable lessons.
Output is strict JSON: schema_version, request_id, capability_fingerprint,
playbook_version, plan_id, ordered catalog-action keys and finite gate identifiers.
Reject unknown fields, duplicate or unavailable keys, invalid types, unauthorized
plans, stale capabilities, unknown gates, and out-of-arm choices. Do not permit
executable conditions or model-selected raw spell IDs/coordinates.

Keep original server capture time immutable. The sidecar must not move capture
timestamps forward to make old observations appear fresh. The existing v1 adapter's
capture-offset behavior is not suitable evidence of freshness for tactical execution.
Use a server-owned deadline and live revalidation. Negotiate v2 explicitly; v1
personality/chat remain supported and cannot accidentally execute v2 tactics.

Initial bounded budgets (configurable within tested limits):

- One shared model inference at a time, including conversation/planning/reflection;
  coordinate with the existing local model broker/mutex. Never load a second model.
- At most one tactical request per party per 5 seconds; event-driven, not per tick.
- Tactical deadline 2 seconds; accepted plan expires after at most 5 seconds or a
  material state/order/capability change. Default behavior continues while waiting.
- Request <=16 KiB, response <=4 KiB; one pending tactical snapshot per party,
  newer snapshot supersedes older pending work.
- Reflect only out of combat, coalescing up to five finalized summaries; at most
  one review per 60 seconds, deadline 10 seconds, pending review queue <=8.
- Reflection never blocks urgent planning. If inference cannot be cancelled,
  cap its deadline, defer subsequent review and measure contention honestly.
- Bounded telemetry: <=512 events per encounter; aggregate overflow counters,
  mark incomplete evidence and exclude it from promotion. No silent sample loss.

Measure model feasibility before enabling live trials. Repeated deadline misses
mean baseline play with status 'model unavailable/too slow', not broader deadlines
silently allowing stale tactics. Shared coding-worker sessions must not compete
with a live model evaluation; serialize them through the existing mechanism.

# 5. Encounter recorder and authoritative metrics

An encounter starts when Bram or his party engages a relevant hostile while Bram
is enrolled, online and in the owner's party. Track a unique server-generated ID.
End after 5 continuous seconds with no party combat and no live engaged hostiles.
Death/wipe, party leave, owner loss, map change, shutdown or a 5-minute cap finalize
with an explicit end reason; interrupted/incomplete encounters are not successes.

Record selected tactic, policy/model/prompt versions, arm assignment, actual policy
exposure, action selected, cast attempted, engine rejection, completion/effect where
observable, threat/victim transitions, damage, rage and owner intervention. Cast
acceptance is not successful effect application. Unsupported attribution is unknown.

Required coded metrics:

- unprotected_fraction: hostile-time attacking another party member divided by
  hostile-time attacking any party member (seconds summed per engaged hostile).
- recovery_latency_ms: time from a relevant victim switch until return to Bram;
  unresolved switches are censored failures, not zero-latency recoveries.
- rage_blocked_recovery_count: verified recovery opportunities blocked by rage;
  cooldown/range/stance failures are separate. If none exist, do not claim benefit.
- party/Bram deaths, party damage taken, critical-health time, encounter duration,
  rage waste at cap, attempted/rejected/wasted abilities and fallback duration.
- Input integrity, missing events, inference rejection/latency and owner overrides.

Every candidate declares one primary outcome and fixed safety constraints before it
is assigned. Tank-protection candidates use `unprotected_fraction` and recovery
latency. Current-level rotation candidates use effective hostile damage per active
combat second and action-effect rate, while preserving all safety constraints. Do
not reuse a protection metric for a candidate that cannot causally affect protection.

Snapshots carry capability/gear/level fingerprints, party role composition, enemy
template/relative level/count, starting resource bands and policy versions. Ordinary
owner damage/healing is recorded context. Explicit tactical overrides interrupt the
trial; keep their outcome for safety reporting while excluding efficacy comparisons.

# 6. Automatic evaluation during play

Modes: disabled, observe, shadow, trial, paused. Default is disabled until owner
enrollment. Enroll into observe; collect at least 20 complete eligible encounters
in a matching context before shadow. Shadow requires at least 10 valid proposals
with zero authority violations before a candidate can enter live trial. Shadow
does not establish counterfactual survival or effectiveness.

Eligibility: supported capabilities, normal outdoor PvE, at most two engaged
non-elite/non-boss hostiles within the declared tested level range, same party
composition and capability/gear fingerprint, all party members alive and initially
at least 80% health, healer resource at least 60% when applicable. BL-001 must pin
enemy level bounds from validated fixtures; unknown difficulty is ineligible.
No PvP, dungeons, active recovery, owner override or death within the previous
10 minutes. Unsafe state changes immediately terminate candidate exposure.

One candidate per Bram/context. Preassign baseline/candidate 1:1 in randomized
blocks within matching strata, persist assignment before exposure, and report all
assigned encounters including failures. Excluded/incomplete reasons remain visible.
No model discretion over assignment and no repeated favorable subset selection.

Initial promotion rule is deliberately conservative and versioned:

- Evaluate efficacy once at 30 complete encounters per arm in a comparable stratum;
  inspect safety continuously. No repeated significance peeking or optional stopping.
- The predeclared primary metric must improve by at least 10% relatively and have
  a 95% bootstrap interval for the mean difference entirely in the favorable
  direction. Resample encounter records, never individual tick samples; persist
  evaluator version and random seed. A zero/undefined baseline is inconclusive.
- Candidate party damage/second and median encounter duration must each be <=110%
  of baseline; retain separate death and emergency-recovery constraints.
- Any candidate-arm party death, suppressed emergency recovery, new pull, unauthorized
  action or owner hold violation immediately suspends trials and restores baseline.
  This is a conservative stop, not a claim that the candidate caused the death.
- Insufficient comparability, sample count or statistical evidence yields inconclusive.
  After 100 eligible encounters or 14 days without a decision, archive the trial as
  inconclusive; do not repeatedly retest the same candidate to obtain a lucky pass.

These thresholds are initial product gates, not proof of causal improvement under
all live conditions. Correlated play sessions and owner contributions limit inference.
Report per-session breakdowns and run a paired disposable fixture before enabling a
candidate family. If that evidence conflicts with live outcomes, retain baseline.

Persist retained playbooks atomically and monitor the same safety stops afterward.
Level, relevant gear, learned ability or policy implementation changes invalidate
context eligibility; retain history, return to observation for the new fingerprint.
Promotion must be meaningful: verify accepted settings actually reach action selection.

# 7. Memory, storage and recovery

Use the character database's ordered migrations, with new names selected at
implementation time. Do not modify applied migrations. Proposed logical tables:

- bot_learning_profile: character key, owner-controlled mode, schema and active version.
- bot_learning_playbook: immutable version, parent, context fingerprint, settings,
  provenance, accepted/rolled-back state and timestamps.
- bot_learning_encounter: unique encounter ID, context, arm, metrics and integrity.
- bot_learning_candidate: bounded change, cited evidence IDs, state and evaluator version.
- bot_learning_audit: enroll/pause/override/promotion/rollback and reason.

Game-side writer owns mutations and owner authorization. The Python sidecar receives
bounded memory values and returns proposals; it receives no database credentials.
Idempotent writes use encounter/request IDs; duplicate delivery cannot double count.
Activation uses compare-and-swap on expected playbook version; restart cannot activate
half-written candidates. Startup marks unfinished encounters interrupted.

Retain at most 500 summaries per companion for 30 days; preserve compact aggregate
evidence for retained/rolled-back versions and at most 20 playbook versions. Pending
candidate evidence is pinned within the total budget; if exhausted, pause the trial
instead of deleting its basis. Bound diagnostic events separately to 7 days/local
storage, opt-in. Purging evidence must not fabricate remaining sample counts.
DB failure disables evaluation/promotion and falls back to baseline; no unbounded
in-memory backlog. Rollback never alters XP, inventory, spells, quests or money.

# 8. Owner controls and visible behavior

Add owner-only SEC_PLAYER commands using existing command/ownership patterns:

- .botlearn start Bram: enroll in observe; automatic shadow/trial progression once gates pass.
- .botlearn status Bram: mode, capability window, active/candidate versions, sample counts,
  exclusions, metrics and next gate; explicit 'not enough evidence' when appropriate.
- .botlearn pause Bram: stop inference/trials and use established baseline; retain history.
- .botlearn resume Bram: recheck capabilities and resume eligible state; never bypass gates.
- .botlearn explain Bram: brief evidence-linked lesson/experiment summary.
- .botlearn rollback Bram: restore previous accepted version or baseline, cancel candidate.

Existing hold/orders always win and invalidate outstanding proposals. Leaving the
party stops planning/recording exposure; rejoin creates a fresh session. Learning
identity survives rename and rejoin. No unsolicited per-fight chatter; provide a
rate-limited promotion/rollback notice and an on-demand session summary. Do not
announce learning when only personality text changed.

# 9. Ordered local-LLM work cards

Each row is a separate fresh bounded assignment, not a request to implement the
whole PRD at once. Head verifies current files/hashes and supplies the existing
docs/bots/ralph/worker-card.md evidence card before every dispatch. Proposed new
module names are design suggestions; exact allowed paths are fixed by each card.

| Card | Deliverable and likely scope | Required acceptance evidence |
| --- | --- | --- |
| BL-001 | Read-only capability/experiment audit; Tank/Combat and relevant AI functions plus exact game-data rows | Legal rage spender, units, capability windows, baseline path, fixture definitions and stop/prerequisite decision |
| BL-001A | Shared catalog/playbook/decision contracts and pure baseline extraction; bounded Combat/class-action code | Warrior plus mage baseline-equivalence value tests, preserved priority/eligibility, incompatible playbook fallback; no learned behavior |
| BL-001B | Integrate common policy interface into Assist/ContinueCombat/Defend, retaining source-specific semantics | Below/above tank gate matrix, .botinit defend path, Taunt bypass preserved explicitly, default behavior parity and decision applicability traces |
| BL-002 | Pure encounter/value contracts and recorder hooks; new Companion/Encounter module | Boundaries, attribution, caps, overflow, deaths, interruption, deterministic metrics; no behavior change |
| BL-003 | Ordered memory migration, bounded async persistence and version lifecycle | Isolated DB migration twice, duplicate delivery, writer failure, restart, rollback and retention tests |
| BL-004 | Tactical protocol v2, fake adapter and validator; preserve v1 | Invalid/stale/reordered/capability-change/owner-hold tests; immutable timestamps; bounded queues |
| BL-001R | Current-level warrior rotation-candidate audit after the catalog exists | Capability fingerprint, one priority/gate delta, causal metric bundle, legal fixture and explicit no-candidate result if unsupported |
| BL-005 | Warrior finite-priority plan through the accepted shared interface | Current-level legal ability ordering/gates, unchanged inapplicable paths, baseline parity, owner/emergency preemption and unsupported-ability tests; no silent new rotation mechanics |
| BL-006 | Real planner/reflection prompts and memory selection | Strict schemas, fake inference, fabricated evidence rejection, latency/contention measurement, no direct DB authority |
| BL-007 | Candidate evaluator, matching, assignment and auto mode transitions | Synthetic improvement/no effect/regression, sparse/zero baseline, exclusions, no repeated peeking, restart and atomic activation |
| BL-008 | Owner commands, notices, status and explain | Ownership/intruder, renamed character, party/session boundaries, pause/resume/rollback tests |
| BL-009 | Cumulative disposable runtime and controlled real-model qualification | Actual behavior difference, off-line fallback, two-boot persistence, engine metrics vs recorder, model budgets |
| BL-010 | Owner party-play pilot and final report | Normal .botinit session, real observations, evidence-backed status, restart continuity; honest inconclusive results allowed |

Execution order is BL-001 (closed: reserve rejected) -> BL-001A -> BL-001B ->
BL-001R -> BL-002 through BL-010. BL-001A/B are prerequisite refactor work,
independently accepted before learning is connected. BL-001R establishes a viable
current-level rotation candidate; it does not wait for Taunt or level 10. If an
audit identifies a required gameplay capability addition, the head defines a
separate bounded card and accepts its new baseline before BL-005; workers cannot
silently expand scope.
Dependencies are sequential unless the head provides an explicitly independent
bounded assignment. Each executable candidate must resolve viability before it is
tested, but baseline extraction and observe-only recording may proceed without a
candidate. Do not mark the feature finished at shadow-only recording.
BL-009 qualifies automated trials; BL-010 establishes in-game usability. Measured
improvement is a separate claim and requires the promotion evidence above.

Worker rules: one serialized park-agent request, one editing worker per worktree;
no nested model sessions, retrieval calls, commits, staging, pushes, deployment,
Jira transitions or final acceptance. Read only assigned source/synthetic evidence;
no .env, private character records, backups or raw personal logs. Report actual
tests, hashes, assumptions and failures. Standalone park-head follows its separate
head rules and must not launch a nested worker. Occupied/unavailable model is a
reported fallback, not permission to start a second inference server.

# 10. Validation and release boundaries

Use value tests for recorder/policy/evaluator contracts and disposable Compose
projects with separate verified volumes for world/database integration. Never seed
fixtures into tortoise-local_database. Every runtime report identifies source,
image, fixture identity, model/prompt/evaluator versions and commands actually run.

Relevant repository gates:

    python -m unittest discover -s docker -p 'test_*.py'
    python -m py_compile docker/server.py
    docker compose config --quiet
    git diff --check
    docker compose build world

Full discovery/runtime gates require the documented disposable setup. Run targeted
new suites per card, then cumulative relevant gates; do not claim skipped integration
tests passed. Preserve existing BUILD_JOBS and ALLOW_TURTLE_ADDONS. Parser-check any
changed shell/PowerShell scripts. Build success alone is not behavior acceptance.

Mandatory scenarios: unchanged baseline with feature disabled; absent/slow/malformed
model; old response after hold; party leave/rejoin; owner logout; unsupported low-level
warrior; ability/gear change; model requests illegal ability; candidate suppresses
emergency action; event overflow; duplicate summary; DB unavailable; crash during
activation; migration retry; restart restores accepted policy; rollback preserves
character state; sparse encounters never produce invented improvement.

Refactor-specific gates: source-by-source parity for Assist/ContinueCombat/Defend;
warrior current-level and below/above the Taunt capability gate; actual .botinit defend flow; unchanged
default spell ordering, rank resolution and on-next-swing exclusion; mage value-level
baseline parity; class/role-incompatible playbook rejection; resource units; owner
orders and emergency actions unaffected by tuning. Verify candidate exposure reaches
the selected action path and changes a legal choice in an eligible fixture. A saved
reserve value or schema-valid LLM response alone is not evidence of dynamic control.

Measure disabled versus observe versus trial world-tick overhead under the same
fixture. Initial gate: p95 processing increase <=2 ms and no sustained tick-cadence
regression, using at least 10-minute windows and reporting p99, sample count and
machine load. Measure inference latency and deadline acceptance separately; the
single historical Phase 2 model round is not a tactical performance benchmark.

Before any later authorized personal rollout: complete BL-009, back up, check online
sessions, apply additive migrations through the normal updater, and begin with Bram
in observe mode. No live deployment is performed by this PRD task. Keep feature
disable/rollback usable without removing character data or reverting the database.

Final report distinguishes implemented behavior, deterministic/synthetic evidence,
real-model evidence, owner client observations, pending acceptance and actual
improvement. Retrieval services were restored on 2026-09-21; verify status and
freshness before each substantial card, sync every tracked-source change, and report
its embedding and derived-write counts. Do not change existing Phase 2 acceptance
flags as a side effect.
